{"version":1,"reference":{"kind":"artifact_content","namespace":"3451","artifact_type":"feature-context","name":"plan/feature-context-layer-attribution.md@sha256:a07bdf7bd95b29878c2dc98185397020c9dc8a68e784a6aad2ff05a89cbfa08e"},"owner_reference":"3451","content":"# ARTIFACT:DISCOVERY\n\n## Feature\n\nLayer attribution on a defect record, so a fix lands where the cause lives rather than where the symptom appeared.\n\n## Problem Statement\n\nA plugin in this repo is three things at once: code, prose instructions that sometimes call the tools that code exposes, and a harness whose hooks, models and tool-calling semantics are also known. A defect can originate in any of them. Nothing on a backlog item records which layer is which, so the layer where a symptom is *noticed* becomes the layer where the fix is written. The fix then removes the instance and leaves the class, and the same defect recurs somewhere the first fix never reached.\n\nThe failure is measured, not theorised. `scope.md` names two prose labels; across 21 items filed in one session `**Hypothesis**` appeared in 0 of 21, and 15 of 21 wrote the other label unbolded. A rule stated in prose, with nothing refusing non-conforming input, produced zero compliance.\n\nSix worked instances are recorded on the item, three from the session that filed it and three from 2026-09-15. The 2026-09-15 set is the sharpest available evidence because in one of them the correction already existed in the backlog \u2014 #3446 records that both non-Claude harnesses support plugin-bundled hooks, and #3437 opens by calling the opposite claim false \u2014 and the fix was still written as prose at the layer where the symptom appeared, by an agent that did not find either issue first.\n\n## Goals\n\n1. A defect record distinguishes the layer where the problem was observed from the layer that caused it, as data a consumer can read rather than as prose a reader must interpret.\n2. The distinction is produced by something that refuses or flags a non-conforming record, not by a convention stated in prose \u2014 prose scored 0 of 21 on the comparable rule.\n3. A record can express \"cause layer not yet determined\" at the point where the cause is genuinely unknown, without compelling the filer to guess.\n4. A record states which consumers traverse the layer a fix targets, so a fix placed where only some callers read it is visible as such before it ships.\n5. A wrong layer attribution is reviewable. The item's own scope note concedes a validator cannot check correctness; the gain sought is that a wrong answer becomes inspectable where an absent field is not.\n\n## Anti-Goals\n\n1. Enforcing that a named layer is *correct*. The six recorded failures were made by people and agents capable of filling in a form.\n2. Adding another prose convention. That approach has a measured 0-of-21 result on the directly comparable rule.\n3. Compelling speculation at creation time. `create/scope.md` bans unlabelled causal claims at creation and assigns cause verification to grooming; a required cause field at creation would force exactly what that rule forbids.\n4. Re-deriving the layer taxonomy from scratch. The item supplies it.\n5. Retrofitting attribution onto the existing backlog. Nothing in the item asks for a migration.\n\n## Requirements\n\n### Functional\n\n1. A defect record carries the observed layer and the causing layer such that a consumer reading the record can tell them apart without parsing prose.\n2. Creation succeeds for a record whose causing layer is not yet determined, and that undetermined state is distinguishable from an omitted field.\n3. A defined point in the item lifecycle detects a record that lacks the distinction and does not let it pass silently.\n4. A record identifies the consumers that traverse the layer its fix targets.\n5. The lifecycle point that detects non-conformance reports what was missing, in terms that name the record and the absent attribution.\n\n### Non-Functional\n\n1. Compliance is measurable as a rate against the existing 0-of-21 prose baseline, using the same counting method, so the change can be shown to have worked or not.\n2. No information is demanded at a lifecycle stage where that information does not yet exist \u2014 the creation stage already omits Story and Acceptance Criteria on exactly this reasoning.\n3. Detection surfaces a signal the caller receives. A silent mis-key is the failure mode already open as #2974, where content stored under an `unknown__` key reads back as absent with no signal.\n\n## Design Questions for Planning\n\n<!-- User-supplied design proposals. Signals for the architect's research phase, not commitments. -->\n\n1. The item proposes a five-row table: layer observed, layer that produced the artifact, layer that caused it, layer the fix targets, layer that enforces the rule afterwards. Evaluate whether five rows or fewer \u2014 the item's own open question records that rows 1 and 2 coincide in most defects, and that a substantially redundant form gets filled in mechanically, which is the failure mode of a skimmed instruction wearing a schema.\n2. The item proposes the attribution live in the backlog item schema as a field the groom gate validates in code. Evaluate placement against `create/scope.md`'s ban on unlabelled causal claims at creation \u2014 creation, groom, both, or a third point.\n3. The item proposes \"a validator that refuses the item\". Evaluate refusal against flagging, against a required-but-nullable field, and against a warning the caller already receives \u2014 noting that a warning nobody reads is the #2974 failure and a refusal that fires wrongly blocks filing.\n4. Evaluate whether the existing `**Hypothesis**:` marker already supplies the creation-time expression for an undetermined cause layer, given it is the one creation marker with a reader (`finalize.md` gates on it, `swarm.md` matches it back by exact text).\n5. Evaluate whether row 5's sub-question \u2014 which callers traverse that layer \u2014 is its own field or is derivable once the layer taxonomy is named.\n6. Evaluate whether the enforcement point belongs in the same code path as the existing section-registry check, which already warns on an unregistered section name and is already known to be ignored downstream.\n\n## References\n\n- #3451 \u2014 this item, including its six-row evidence table and its open questions for grooming\n- #3446 \u2014 both harnesses support plugin-bundled hooks; packaging gap, not harness limit\n- #3437 \u2014 closed, refiled as #3446; opens by calling the opposite claim false\n- #2974 \u2014 P0: `unknown__`-keyed content reads back as absent with no signal; the silent-detection failure mode this work must not repeat\n- #2973 \u2014 description-embedded `##` headings promoted on a GitHub round trip; 136 of 663 open issues affected, measured 2026-09-15\n- `plugins/development-harness/skills/work-backlog-item/references/workflows/create/scope.md` \u2014 Step 0 classification and the creation-stage strip rules\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/section_registry.py` \u2014 the existing registry whose unregistered-name warning is the nearest working precedent\n- `plugins/development-harness/backlog_core/parsing.py` \u2014 `build_issue_body`'s docstring, the precedent for deliberately omitting a section that is a later-stage output\n\n## Resolved Questions\n\n| Question | Answer | Source |\n|----------|--------|--------|\n| Is the prose-convention approach already tried? | Yes. `scope.md` names two labels; `**Hypothesis**` appeared in 0 of 21 items, and 15 of 21 wrote the other label unbolded. | #3451 body, repo owner's measurement |\n| Is the layer taxonomy already defined? | Yes \u2014 code, prose instructions that call the code's tools, and the harness. Supplied by the repo owner. | #3451 body |\n| Does a precedent exist for omitting a field at creation because it is a later-stage output? | Yes. `build_issue_body` deliberately generates no Story and no Acceptance Criteria, because both are grooming outputs established from information absent at intake. | `backlog_core/parsing.py` docstring |\n| Does a precedent exist for detecting a non-conforming section name? | Yes. The section registry warns on an unregistered name and stores under an `unknown__` key. | `backlog_core/section_registry.py`; observed live on #3451, 2026-09-15 |\n| Is a detection-without-consumption failure already known? | Yes, and it is P0. Content under an `unknown__` key reads back as absent with `section_filter_miss: false`. | #2974 |\n| Has the class recurred with the correction already on file? | Yes. A prose fix for a harness-delivery gap was written on 2026-09-15 while #3446 and #3437 already held the correction. | #3446, #3437, and #3451's evidence table |\n\n## Open Questions\n\n- None blocking. The placement question, the row-count question, and the refuse-versus-flag question are design decisions with multiple valid options, routed to Design Questions for Planning rather than escalated.\n\n## User Confirmation\n\n- [x] AUTO_MODE active \u2014 treated as confirmed per the discovery gate."}